  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=\\frac{10}{(t+1)^{2}},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=4$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{10}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\int (t+1)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{(t+1)^{-1}}{-1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(0)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =-\\frac{10}{0+1}+C \\\\ 4 & =-10+C \\\\ C & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\frac{10}{t+1}+14.\n$$",
